; C13d  No page states it. The gate reads Winning 42 p. 44 (W42-p044-c, a
; rule about discarding) as a rule about leading.
; Rule, as the gate reads it: a setter on lead leads a non-trump, non-count
; tile that is his only tile of the suit it leads, to make himself void.
; The book's own setter-lead rules (pp. 46-48, W42-p046-j, W42-p048-c: lead
; what calls for count) cut against it, and its one incidental instance (Hand
; 29, p. 101) gives another reason. In Hand 28 (p. 98) the gate fires and the
; book leads otherwise (tests/book.rs).
; Python gate (pour_setter gate_c13d): the same.
; How this encoding differs: it cannot say "his only tile of that suit" and
; allows every non-trump, non-count lead: the gate's superset.
; GAP declarer: the bidder is written in as S0 (only to name the setters).
; GAP none: "the only tile I hold that follows the suit it leads" is a negated
; existential.
(policy c13d-lead-a-singleton
  (initial play)
  (fallback lowest-legal)
  (rule lead-the-lone-tile (in play) (next play) (select answer)
    (guard
      (query
        (roles (chair me) (domino lone) (context suit))
        (out lone)
        (pattern
          (viewer me)
          (opponent me S0)
          (leader me)
          (next-actor me)
          (own-legal lone)
          (not (in lone q*))
          (count lone 0)
          (leads-context lone suit)
          ; GAP none: (none (own-legal other) (in other suit)) for another tile other
          )))))
